MIPS3's DRC already knows how to turn a guest load/store into an inline bounds
check plus a single host load ("fastram"), but nothing in src/mame/sgi ever
registered a region, so every guest memory access on indy_4610 went out of line
into the generic memory-system dispatch. Read straight out of `-drc_log_native`,
the mode-2 read32 stub was 10 instructions of vtlb translation followed by 22
instructions of dispatch -- an `ldmxcsr`, an address swizzle, a rip-relative
dispatch-table base, a dependent table load, a vtable load, an INDIRECT VIRTUAL
`call` into `handler_entry_read_memory<3,0>::read`, and a second `ldmxcsr` to
restore FP mode -- before the `ret`. Loads are 36.6% and stores 6.0% of the
compiled guest instruction stream (IRIX's 64-bit register save/restore alone
makes `ld` 23.3%), so that dispatch is paid 0.426 times per guest instruction.

With the region registered the same stub ends in

    cmp ebx,0Bffffffh / ja / cmp ebx,8000000h / jb / xor ebx,4 / movsxd / mov / ret

TWO BUGS HAD TO BE FIXED FIRST, BOTH IN static_generate_fastram_accessor.

The host buffer behind a fastram region is laid out by the MEMORY SYSTEM, for
the width and endianness of the CPU's program space. The generator hardcoded the
32-bit-space swizzles (BYTE4_XOR_BE/WORD_XOR_BE, and a dword-pair `DROR` for
qwords) while the interpreter fastram path in mips3.cpp has always used
m_byte_xor/m_word_xor/m_dword_xor, which are 64-bit-aware. On a 64-bit
big-endian space -- R4600BE and R5000BE, i.e. every SGI IP22/IP24 machine -- a
byte lives at host offset `A^7` not `A^3`, a dword at `A^4` not `A`, and a guest
qword already occupies one host qword and must NOT be half-rotated. Registering
fastram without this fix returns scrambled bytes. The generator now uses the
device's own xors and emits the dword-pair rotate only for a 32-bit big-endian
space, so every existing 32-bit fastram user (kinst, the SH2/PPC-style
drivers' MIPS equivalents) emits byte-for-byte the same code as before.

MIPS3_MAX_FASTRAM was 3. A 256 MB Indy configures four 64 MB ranks, and
add_fastram() silently drops anything past the limit, so a quarter of RAM would
have kept falling through to the dispatch path. Consecutive ranks of a bank are
contiguous in both the guest map and the host buffer, so mc.cpp coalesces them
(256 MB becomes two entries, not four) and the limit is raised to 4 for headroom.
Entries are not free: each one adds a cmp/jcc pair to EVERY accessor stub.

Correctness constraints encoded in sgi_mc_device::remap_fastram():

  * The map is rebuilt from scratch on every memcfg write, never incrementally.
    RAM is remapped by the guest at runtime, and a stale host pointer left
    behind by an unmap would be a use-after-free.
  * Only ranges where the installed size fills the configured size are eligible.
    install_ram() MIRRORS a short bank across its configured range and fastram
    has no mirror support, so a mirrored bank must keep falling through.
  * The 0x00000000-0x0007ffff low alias of the first 512 KiB is deliberately not
    registered -- it would be a second entry pointing at an already-registered
    host buffer, for 512 KiB of PROM-era aliasing.
  * DRC self-modifying-code detection is not at risk: mips3 uses entry checksums
    (generate_checksum_block), not write watchpoints, so bypassing the memory
    system on writes changes nothing.
  * HPC3/SCSI DMA writes the same host buffers through the memory system, so
    coherency is preserved.

MEASURED on indy_4610 -gio64_gfx xl24, 256 MB, -video none -nothrottle, one
binary carrying the registration behind an env gate so control and treatment
share the same build. Six interleaved rounds on one pinned core pair, within-run
windowing between emulated-time marks (differencing separate runs is invalid --
IRIX boot diverges from t~120 s), metric emulated_secs/(cycles/2.5e9), median of
PAIRED within-round ratios with a 20k-resample bootstrap CI:

    window (emulated)      control   fastram   paired median   95% CI
    40-120 s  boot/active   58.5%     65.5%       +8.9%        +3.7..+22.6
    120-200 s transition   115.1%    139.5%      +15.7%        +7.4..+36.0
    200-300 s idle/chooser 140.3%    166.9%      +19.1%        +4.7..+23.5

All 18 paired comparisons are positive. The exhibit still does not reach 100% of
real time in the active regime on this change alone -- 58.5% -> 65.5% -- so this
is a large step, not the finish line.

Framebuffer verified, not inferred: the deterministic checkpoint at emulated
t=100 s renders byte-identically to control (PNG md5
0086feeeb211e75d270cae7dffb8a5ce), and a t=600 s soak lands on the iconlogin
chooser with md5 5bbf4cbed33441c8741cd455df1166ed, one of the two frames a
CONTROL boot is already known to produce there.

